# HCW: Numerical Quality of the Integrator

```elixir
project_path = System.get_env("PLUMBLINE_PROJECT_PATH") || Path.join(__DIR__, "..")

Mix.install([{:plumbline, path: project_path}])
```

## What this document is

The other gates ask whether the implementation gets the *physics*
right. This one asks whether it integrates *well* — two properties of
the numerical method that every other check here is structurally unable
to see, because both are invisible at a single step size over a single
orbit.

**Convergence order.** The interface contract names a specific method
of a specific order. An implementation of a *lower*-order method still
satisfies every invariant in the specification notebook: the
trajectories close, the integral is conserved, the oracle agrees — all
of it, just less accurately, and the tolerances there are loose enough
to absorb the difference. Order is only visible by refining the step
and watching how fast the error falls.

**Long-horizon stability.** Every other check runs for one orbit. An
integrator can be perfectly well behaved over one orbit and drift,
ring, or diverge over a hundred. Correctness over a short window is not
stability over a long one, and the difference is exactly the kind of
thing that reaches production intact.

Gated separately, with its own history:

```sh
bin/run_checkpoint livebooks/hcw_numerical_quality.livemd
elixir bin/checkpoint_history.exs hcw_numerical_quality
```

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.join(project_path, "livebooks/checkpoint.exs"))

Checkpoint.init([
  "convergence_error_decreases_under_refinement",
  "convergence_order_matches_the_contract",
  "long_horizon_integral_bounded",
  "long_horizon_orbit_still_closes",
  "long_horizon_drift_not_explosive"
])
```

## Setup

A state with every component active, so no term of the dynamics sits
idle during the measurement.

```elixir
alias Plumbline.Dynamics.HCW

mu = 398_600.4418
a = 6378.137 + 400.0
n = :math.sqrt(mu / (a * a * a))
period = 2 * :math.pi() / n
x0 = 1.0

initial = %HCW.State{x: x0, y: 0.0, z: 0.3, vx: 0.0005, vy: -2 * n * x0, vz: 0.0002}

separation = fn p, q ->
  :math.sqrt(:math.pow(p.x - q.x, 2) + :math.pow(p.y - q.y, 2) + :math.pow(p.z - q.z, 2))
end

%{period_min: Float.round(period / 60, 3)}
```

## Convergence under refinement

Deliberately coarse grids. At the step sizes the specification notebook
uses, the error is already down at the rounding floor, where the
method's order is unmeasurable — refinement stops buying anything and
the measurement reads noise. Order is only observable while truncation
error still dominates.

```elixir
step_counts = [50, 100, 200, 400, 800]

errors =
  for count <- step_counts do
    worst =
      initial
      |> HCW.propagate(n, period / count, period)
      |> Enum.map(fn {t, numeric} -> separation.(numeric, HCW.analytic(initial, n, t)) end)
      |> Enum.max()

    IO.puts("  steps/orbit #{String.pad_leading(to_string(count), 4)}   error #{worst} km")
    worst
  end
```

Refinement must actually buy accuracy. This is separate from the order
check below and comes first, because if any error is zero or fails to
fall, the order is not merely wrong — it is undefined, and the ratio
computed from it would be meaningless rather than red.

```elixir
strictly_decreasing? =
  errors
  |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
  |> Enum.all?(fn [coarse, fine] -> fine > 0.0 and fine < coarse end)

Checkpoint.check("convergence_error_decreases_under_refinement", strictly_decreasing?,
  expected: true,
  actual: strictly_decreasing?,
  unit: "boolean",
  message: "every halving of the step must reduce the error, and no error may be exactly zero"
)
```

The observed order is the base-2 logarithm of the ratio between
successive errors: halving the step and dividing the error by `2^p`
means the method is of order `p`.

```elixir
orders =
  errors
  |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
  |> Enum.map(fn [coarse, fine] -> :math.log(coarse / fine) / :math.log(2) end)

Enum.zip(step_counts, orders)
|> Enum.each(fn {c, p} -> IO.puts("  #{c} -> #{c * 2} steps/orbit   observed order #{Float.round(p, 4)}") end)

worst_order_error = orders |> Enum.map(&abs(&1 - 4.0)) |> Enum.max()

Checkpoint.check("convergence_order_matches_the_contract", worst_order_error < 0.5,
  expected: 4.0,
  actual: orders |> Enum.map(&Float.round(&1, 4)),
  tolerance: 0.5,
  unit: "order",
  message: "observed convergence order at each refinement; a lower-order method still satisfies every other gate in this repository"
)
```

## Long horizon

One hundred orbits. Sampled at each orbit boundary, so the closure and
the drift can be read as series rather than as endpoints.

```elixir
orbits = 100
steps_per_orbit = 1_000
dt = period / steps_per_orbit

long = HCW.propagate(initial, n, dt, orbits * period)

j_initial = HCW.integral(initial, n)
integrals = Enum.map(long, fn {_, s} -> HCW.integral(s, n) end)
integral_spread = (Enum.max(integrals) - Enum.min(integrals)) / abs(j_initial)

closure_at =
  fn k ->
    {_, s} = Enum.at(long, k * steps_per_orbit)
    separation.(s, initial)
  end

IO.puts("  J spread over #{orbits} orbits: #{integral_spread} relative")
IO.puts("  closure at  50 orbits: #{closure_at.(50)} km")
IO.puts("  closure at 100 orbits: #{closure_at.(100)} km")
```

```elixir
Checkpoint.check("long_horizon_integral_bounded", integral_spread < 1.0e-6,
  expected: 0.0,
  actual: integral_spread,
  tolerance: 1.0e-6,
  unit: "relative",
  message: "spread of J across #{orbits} orbits — RK4 is not symplectic, so this is a bound rather than an equality"
)

final_closure = closure_at.(orbits)

Checkpoint.check("long_horizon_orbit_still_closes", final_closure < 1.0e-3,
  expected: 0.0,
  actual: final_closure,
  tolerance: 1.0e-3,
  unit: "km",
  message: "a drift-free orbit must still return to its start after #{orbits} periods, not merely after one"
)
```

Accumulated error is expected — the method is not symplectic and makes
no claim to conserve anything exactly. What must not happen is
*acceleration*: doubling the elapsed time should roughly double the
drift, not square it. A ratio near two is linear growth; a ratio far
above it means the error is feeding on itself.

```elixir
growth = final_closure / closure_at.(div(orbits, 2))

Checkpoint.check("long_horizon_drift_not_explosive", growth < 4.0,
  expected: "~2 (linear)",
  actual: growth,
  tolerance: 4.0,
  unit: "ratio",
  message: "closure error at #{orbits} orbits against #{div(orbits, 2)} orbits; linear accumulation is fine, compounding is not"
)
```

```elixir
Checkpoint.complete()
```
